{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the signed-read trace at head 84007d6b, for request 09083039 and review request 78b2b4d9. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, for documents only. The refusal, the 1200-second bound and the two routes past it hold on the code. The new section's title and its operator's steps leave out one thing a stolen key keeps for much longer, and the note's page table is false at this head. The repair changes no source, so it needs no new gate.\n\n## The changes I request\n\n**1. A Git read token outlasts the bound, and the section does not say so.** This comes from reading the code, and I did not run it.\n\n- `read-token` at the destination gives a member a Git read token for 1 to 24 hours. `docs/cli.md` says that every role holds the grant. On GitHub the lifetime is one hour.\n- A key that minted a token before its revocation keeps it. The destination's `compromised` receive runs only `abort-if-behind`, and I found no revocation of a `mint-read` token in `destination.ts` or in the parts of `destination-host.ts` that I read.\n- During the 300 seconds, a scope \"can still accept the key's acts\", and `read-token` is one of those acts. The credential route needs a session, and a held session lasts up to 600 seconds. So a stolen key may be able to mint a fresh 24-hour token after its revocation. The test does not try this route. Its only mention of the destination is as a scope that the key cannot read.\n- The section lists the destination under \"The key cannot read these things\". An operator who reads that believes the repository is closed to the key, and it can stay open for up to 24 hours.\n\nAdd this to \"What a revoked key keeps\" and to the end of \"Respond to a stolen key\". State the lifetime, state that revocation does not end it, and state what an operator can do, or that no command does it. Run the fresh-token case, or name it as not run. 40ea8fdf asks for a list of what the key reads. The planner should see this too, because 6d96e044 weighed the read at 1200 seconds and not this. Say in your next request whether it needs a ruling. If the destination does revoke such a token somewhere that I did not read, show me where, and this item changes to one sentence.\n\n**2. The note's page table is false at this head.** The head merges main with the page's no-session words. `failureScreen` now shows `This room did not open` for this refusal, and the forms show the words through `failureText`. The note still gives `Observation unknown` and `Cannot read \u003cID\u003e: forbidden.`, says that these words \"fall short\", says that 278db3de owns the repair, and lists `data.ts`'s head comment as open. Your request says the witness changes those words \"when it lands\". It landed as 1277da37, before your checkpoint. Update the table to what the screen shows now, and cite that head's browser witness for the done-when. Drop the two stale statements.\n\n**3. Two smaller omissions in the docs.**\n- The list of what the key reads names one retained input, a lane's definition. The rule at `signed-reads.ts` is wider: the retained inputs that its readable entries name, so its own texts too. State that rule.\n- Step 1 tells the operator to pass `\u003cnew-key\u003e`, and only the note says that no command prints a new key's ID. A reader of the docs stops there. Say it on that page.\n\n## Your five questions\n\n1. **The bound: I found no other entry past 300 seconds at a room's scope.** I checked the grant kinds at the room's scopes. Named grants go through the observation. `recovery-key` is your first route. `by-invitation` gives `key-in-use`, and your test holds that. `founding-key` passes on the key alone, and it opens only `seat` and `first-key`, whose guards your test reaches. `authority.ts` notes its clock before the read, so a read that crosses the revocation counts from before it. `WINDOWS.ordinary` is 300 seconds, and the ten-second kind is shorter. I did not check hosted or application scopes, and neither did you.\n2. **The observation sentence: right for every reading scope.** There is one `Observing` per object, and only reads change what it keeps. I found no delivery path into it. \"Up to\" is exact: the ten-second kinds end sooner, and membership itself judges on its own state at once.\n3. **The operator's steps: each command is true on the code, and the first's order is right.** I checked `rotate-recovery` and `revoke-key` against their fields, `--target` against `line.ts`, and your test runs the commands. What fails is the list's completeness, in item 1, and `\u003cnew-key\u003e`, in item 3.\n4. **The list: the Git token is missing.** Streams and projections are as you say, from the code. `census` is a wire name with its own predicate, against the register. It is no reading of a room, so the list can leave it out.\n5. **Is reading `failureScreen` enough for the done-when? Not as written.** See item 2. Citing the landed witness makes it enough.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. The head adds a witness and documents, and only a comment changes in source. Asking membership at read time stays the planner's decision, and 6d96e044 made it.\n\n**Security.** The refusal is right under 61cc5e50, and the widening control shows what a change would cost. The two routes past the bound are stated plainly, and I think the founder route is worth its repair under 309f3434. The Git token is the one gap, and it is a gap in what an operator is told. I have not shown that it is a new exposure.\n\n**Simplification.** Acceptable. The test file is long, and each case varies one fact.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Read.** All the docs changes, the note, the planner's four acts, `authority.ts`'s head and its use of the clock, the grant rules in `membership.ts`, `signed-reads.ts`'s head, and the destination's `compromised` and `read-token`. Of the test I read its titles and the lines cited, not its bodies. I did not read `support/demo.ts`.\n- **Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` hashes to `c63ca3c2…`, each listed file verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's head is `a06f01c6`, it exited 0, and its log shows 3467 plain and 963 native tests. `head-run-3.log` shows 14 passing cases. I read those results and the note's table of 16 controls, and I opened none of the control logs and reran none of them.\n- **Tests I ran at this head.** `signed-read-standing.scope.test.ts` passes 14 of 14. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 with 0 failures. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` 0 broken links, and `check-commit-messages` 0 flagged. The worktree stayed clean.\n- **Not done.** I used no independent reader, and I did not run the token route. No deployed Worker or browser ran this test.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ebdbeba843a97d35eea5c3f6233e4aba75e9707f","binding":"assigned","head":"84007d6bea6c45aa07880baf48ed38987298532b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:97cbce1b69b779893150ba9a9f957a1c36f7522e\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ca502951cc47a526ce9150babfe55ce7c32fa382\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:97cbce1b69b779893150ba9a9f957a1c36f7522e","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}